      ":question/problem": "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x-14\\pi )=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2\\cdot (-7)\\cdot \\pi )=cos⁡(x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x-14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$"
