      ":question/problem": "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$ for some $x$, then $cos⁡(x-10\\pi )=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-5$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x-10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{1}{2},$ then $cos⁡(x-10\\pi )=\\frac{1}{2}.$"
